# Next.js 16 + React 19 — Full-Stack SaaS Rules

## Stack Declaration

- Next.js 16 (App Router only — no Pages Router)
- React 19
- TypeScript 5.x (strict mode, `"strict": true` in tsconfig)
- Tailwind CSS 4 with `cn()` utility from `@/lib/utils`
- Turbopack (default bundler in Next.js 16)
- Deployment target: Cloudflare Workers via `@opennextjs/cloudflare`

## File Structure Conventions

```
app/
├── layout.tsx              # Root layout (required) — must render <html> and <body>
├── page.tsx                # Home route (/)
├── loading.tsx             # Root loading UI (auto Suspense boundary)
├── error.tsx               # Root error boundary ("use client" required)
├── not-found.tsx           # Root 404 UI
├── global-error.tsx        # Catches errors in root layout ("use client", must render <html>/<body>)
├── (marketing)/            # Route group — no URL impact
│   ├── layout.tsx
│   ├── page.tsx            # Route: /
│   └── about/page.tsx      # Route: /about
├── (app)/                  # Authenticated app route group
│   ├── layout.tsx          # App shell with auth check
│   ├── dashboard/
│   │   ├── page.tsx        # Route: /dashboard
│   │   ├── loading.tsx
│   │   └── error.tsx
│   └── settings/
│       └── page.tsx        # Route: /settings
├── api/
│   └── webhooks/
│       └── route.ts        # Route Handler (named HTTP exports: GET, POST, etc.)
components/
├── ui/                     # Primitives (shadcn/ui)
├── forms/                  # Form components (client)
├── layouts/                # Layout partials (server)
└── shared/                 # Shared components
lib/
├── utils.ts                # cn() and general utilities
├── db.ts                   # Database client
├── env.ts                  # Typed env validation (zod)
├── auth.ts                 # Auth helpers
└── cloudflare.ts           # Cloudflare bindings helpers
actions/                    # Server Actions (co-located or centralized)
proxy.ts                    # Next.js 16 proxy (replaces middleware.ts)
open-next.config.ts         # OpenNext Cloudflare adapter config
wrangler.jsonc              # Cloudflare Workers config
next.config.ts              # Next.js config with cacheComponents enabled
```

### File Naming

- Route files: always lowercase (`page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, `loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`, `route.ts`)
- Components: PascalCase (`UserProfile.tsx`, `DashboardCard.tsx`)
- Utilities and libs: camelCase (`formatDate.ts`, `parseQuery.ts`)
- Server Actions: camelCase verb-noun (`createProject.ts`, `updateProfile.ts`)
- Types: PascalCase in dedicated files or co-located (`types.ts`, inline with component)

### Key File Convention Rules

- A folder becomes a route only when it contains `page.tsx`
- `layout.tsx` persists across navigation (no remount). Use `template.tsx` only when you need remount-on-navigate behavior
- `error.tsx` and `global-error.tsx` MUST be Client Components (`"use client"`)
- `default.tsx` is required in parallel route slots (`@folder`) for unmatched states
- `proxy.ts` lives at project root — replaces `middleware.ts` (deprecated in Next.js 16)
- Route groups `(name)` organize files without affecting URLs
- Dynamic segments: `[id]`, catch-all: `[...slug]`, optional catch-all: `[[...slug]]`
- In Next.js 16, `params` is a `Promise` — always `await params` before use

## Component Model — Server vs Client

### Server Components (default)

Every component in `app/` is a Server Component unless marked otherwise. Prefer server components for:
- Data fetching (direct async/await, no useEffect)
- Heavy computation and data transformation
- Accessing backend resources (DB, filesystem, secrets)
- Rendering static or data-driven UI

```tsx
// app/dashboard/page.tsx — Server Component (default)
import { db } from "@/lib/db";

export default async function DashboardPage() {
  const stats = await db.query.stats.findMany();
  return <StatsGrid stats={stats} />;
}
```

### Client Components

Add `"use client"` ONLY when the component needs:
- React hooks (useState, useEffect, useRef, useActionState, etc.)
- Event handlers (onClick, onSubmit, onChange)
- Browser APIs (window, document, localStorage, IntersectionObserver)
- Third-party client libraries that use hooks or browser APIs

```tsx
"use client";

import { useState } from "react";
import { cn } from "@/lib/utils";

interface CounterProps {
  initialCount?: number;
}

export function Counter({ initialCount = 0 }: CounterProps) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(initialCount);
  return (
    <button
      onClick={() => setCount((c) => c + 1)}
      className={cn("rounded-lg px-4 py-2", count > 10 && "bg-primary text-primary-foreground")}
    >
      {count}
    </button>
  );
}
```

### Critical Rules

- NEVER use `"use client"` on `page.tsx` or `layout.tsx` — extract interactive parts into child Client Components
- Push `"use client"` as far down the tree as possible (leaf components)
- NEVER import server-only code (db, secrets, fs) in Client Components
- NEVER use `getServerSideProps`, `getStaticProps`, or any Pages Router pattern
- NEVER import from `next/router` — use `next/navigation` (`useRouter`, `usePathname`, `useSearchParams`)
- NEVER use `<Head>` from `next/head` — use `metadata` export or `generateMetadata()`

## React 19 Patterns

### Form Handling with useActionState + useFormStatus

```tsx
"use client";

import { useActionState } from "react";
import { useFormStatus } from "react-dom";
import { createProject } from "@/actions/createProject";

function SubmitButton() {
  const { pending } = useFormStatus();
  return (
    <button type="submit" disabled={pending}>
      {pending ? "Creating..." : "Create Project"}
    </button>
  );
}

export function CreateProjectForm() {
  const [state, formAction, isPending] = useActionState(createProject, null);

  return (
    <form action={formAction}>
      <input name="name" required />
      {state?.error && <p className="text-destructive">{state.error}</p>}
      <SubmitButton />
    </form>
  );
}
```

### Key React 19 Rules

- `useActionState` replaces deprecated `useFormState` — import from `"react"`, not `"react-dom"`
- `useFormStatus` must be called in a child component rendered INSIDE the `<form>`, not in the form component itself
- Server Action functions receive `(prevState, formData)` when used with `useActionState` — the first arg is always previous state
- `useOptimistic` for instant UI feedback before server responds
- `use()` hook to unwrap Promises and Context in render — replaces some useEffect patterns
- Refs can be passed as regular props (no `forwardRef` wrapper needed in React 19)
- `<Context>` shorthand replaces `<Context.Provider>`

## Data Fetching

### Server Components (primary pattern)

```tsx
export default async function BlogPage() {
  const posts = await db.query.posts.findMany({
    orderBy: desc(posts.createdAt),
    limit: 20,
  });
  return <PostList posts={posts} />;
}
```

### Parallel Data Fetching

```tsx
export default async function DashboardPage() {
  const [stats, recentActivity, notifications] = await Promise.all([
    getStats(),
    getRecentActivity(),
    getNotifications(),
  ]);
  return (
    <>
      <StatsGrid stats={stats} />
      <ActivityFeed items={recentActivity} />
      <NotificationList notifications={notifications} />
    </>
  );
}
```

### Rules

- Fetch data in Server Components with async/await — NEVER use useEffect + fetch + useState for initial data loads
- Use `Promise.all` for independent parallel requests — avoid sequential waterfalls
- For client-side real-time/polling data, use TanStack Query (React Query) — never raw useEffect + fetch
- Use `loading.tsx` for route-level Suspense boundaries
- Use `<Suspense>` with fallback for component-level streaming

## Next.js 16 Cache Components

Enable in `next.config.ts`:
```ts
const nextConfig: NextConfig = {
  cacheComponents: true,
};
```

### `"use cache"` Directive

```tsx
"use cache";

import { cacheLife, cacheTag } from "next/cache";

export async function getProducts() {
  cacheLife("hours");
  cacheTag("products");
  const products = await db.query.products.findMany();
  return products;
}
```

### Cache Invalidation

- `updateTag(tag)` — Server Actions only. Immediate cache expiry + fresh data in same request. Use for user-facing mutations (read-your-writes).
- `revalidateTag(tag, profile)` — Server Actions and Route Handlers. Stale-while-revalidate. Use `'max'` profile for most cases.
- `revalidatePath(path)` — unchanged from previous model.

```tsx
"use server";

import { updateTag, revalidateTag } from "next/cache";

export async function updateProduct(formData: FormData) {
  await db.update(products).set({ name: formData.get("name") });
  updateTag("products"); // User sees change immediately
}

// In a webhook Route Handler:
export async function POST(request: Request) {
  await processWebhook(request);
  revalidateTag("products", "max"); // Background revalidation is fine
}
```

### Built-in cacheLife Profiles

| Profile     | stale   | revalidate | expire  |
|-------------|---------|------------|---------|
| `default`   | 0       | 15 min     | never   |
| `seconds`   | 0       | 1 s        | 60 s    |
| `minutes`   | 5 min   | 1 min      | 1 hr    |
| `hours`     | 5 min   | 1 hr       | 1 day   |
| `days`      | 5 min   | 1 day      | 1 week  |
| `weeks`     | 5 min   | 1 week     | 1 month |
| `max`       | 5 min   | 1 month    | never   |

### Rules

- NEVER use `export const dynamic`, `export const revalidate`, or `export const fetchCache` — they error with cacheComponents
- Always set an explicit `cacheLife` profile in every `"use cache"` scope
- Dynamic request APIs (`cookies()`, `headers()`, `params`, `searchParams`) MUST be read outside `"use cache"` scopes — wrap in `<Suspense>` boundaries
- Components that read request-time data become "dynamic holes" in the static shell
- Use `cacheTag()` to label data for targeted invalidation
- Prefer `updateTag()` in Server Actions for mutations the user should see immediately
- Prefer `revalidateTag(tag, 'max')` for background refresh (webhooks, admin changes, catalog updates)

## Server Actions

```tsx
// actions/createProject.ts
"use server";

import { z } from "zod";
import { db } from "@/lib/db";
import { updateTag } from "next/cache";
import { auth } from "@/lib/auth";

const schema = z.object({
  name: z.string().min(1).max(100),
  description: z.string().max(500).optional(),
});

type ActionState = {
  success: boolean;
  error?: string;
  data?: { id: string };
} | null;

export async function createProject(
  prevState: ActionState,
  formData: FormData
): Promise<ActionState> {
  const session = await auth();
  if (!session) return { success: false, error: "Unauthorized" };

  const parsed = schema.safeParse({
    name: formData.get("name"),
    description: formData.get("description"),
  });

  if (!parsed.success) {
    return { success: false, error: parsed.error.issues[0].message };
  }

  try {
    const [project] = await db.insert(projects).values({
      ...parsed.data,
      ownerId: session.user.id,
    }).returning();

    updateTag("projects");
    return { success: true, data: { id: project.id } };
  } catch {
    return { success: false, error: "Failed to create project. Please try again." };
  }
}
```

### Rules

- Always validate input with zod — Server Actions receive untrusted data
- Always authenticate/authorize before processing
- Return typed result objects — NEVER throw errors to the client for expected failures
- When used with `useActionState`, the first parameter is always `prevState`
- Use `updateTag()` for cache invalidation in Server Actions (read-your-writes)
- Server Actions are POST requests — suitable for mutations, not reads

## Route Handlers (API Routes)

```tsx
// app/api/webhooks/stripe/route.ts
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from "next/server";
import { z } from "zod";

export async function POST(request: NextRequest) {
  const signature = request.headers.get("stripe-signature");
  if (!signature) {
    return NextResponse.json({ error: "Missing signature" }, { status: 401 });
  }

  try {
    const body = await request.json();
    // ... process webhook
    return NextResponse.json({ received: true }, { status: 200 });
  } catch {
    return NextResponse.json({ error: "Invalid request" }, { status: 400 });
  }
}
```

### Rules

- Route Handlers live in `app/api/` as `route.ts` files
- Export named functions matching HTTP methods: `GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, `DELETE`
- Always validate request body with zod
- Always return proper HTTP status codes
- Use `NextRequest` and `NextResponse` types
- Authenticate before processing — check session or verify webhook signatures
- For cache invalidation in Route Handlers, use `revalidateTag(tag, 'max')` (NOT `updateTag`)

## Proxy (replaces Middleware)

```ts
// proxy.ts — project root
import { NextResponse } from "next/server";
import type { NextRequest } from "next/server";

export function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
  const { pathname } = request.nextUrl;

  if (pathname.startsWith("/dashboard")) {
    const token = request.cookies.get("session");
    if (!token) {
      return NextResponse.redirect(new URL("/login", request.url));
    }
  }

  return NextResponse.next();
}

export const config = {
  matcher: ["/dashboard/:path*", "/settings/:path*"],
};
```

### Rules

- `proxy.ts` at project root replaces `middleware.ts` (deprecated in Next.js 16)
- Export function is named `proxy()`, not `middleware()`
- Keep proxy logic minimal — auth redirects, locale detection, header injection
- NEVER do heavy computation, database queries, or data fetching in the proxy
- Run `npx @next/codemod@latest upgrade` to auto-migrate from middleware.ts

## TypeScript Strict Mode

- NEVER use `any` — use `unknown` and narrow with type guards
- NEVER use `@ts-ignore` or `@ts-expect-error` without an explaining comment
- NEVER use `as Type` casts without a guard function verifying the shape
- All exported functions MUST have explicit return types
- Use `interface` for object shapes with extension points, `type` for unions/intersections/utility types
- Props interfaces are named `{ComponentName}Props`
- Prefer `as const` assertions and template literal types over enums
- Validate all external data boundaries (API responses, form data, env vars) with zod

```ts
// lib/env.ts — typed environment variables
import { z } from "zod";

const envSchema = z.object({
  DATABASE_URL: z.string().url(),
  AUTH_SECRET: z.string().min(32),
  NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL: z.string().url(),
});

export const env = envSchema.parse(process.env);
```

## Error Handling

### Result Pattern for Server Actions

```ts
type ActionResult<T = void> =
  | { success: true; data: T }
  | { success: false; error: string };
```

### Error Boundaries

- `error.tsx` per route segment for localized error recovery with `reset()` function
- `global-error.tsx` at app root for catastrophic errors (must render own `<html>` and `<body>`)
- Both MUST be Client Components
- Use `<Suspense>` boundaries to isolate failures in streaming scenarios

### Rules

- Return user-friendly error messages — NEVER expose stack traces, SQL errors, or internal details
- Log errors with structured context (request ID, user ID, action name) server-side
- Use toast notifications for recoverable client-side errors
- Use error pages for unrecoverable errors (404, 500)

## Performance Best Practices

- Server Components by default — they produce zero client JS
- Use `next/dynamic` with `{ ssr: false }` for heavy client-only components (charts, editors)
- Use `next/image` for all images with explicit `width`/`height` or `fill` — use `priority` only for above-the-fold (LCP) images
- Use `next/font` for font loading — NEVER load fonts from external CDNs in `<head>`
- Prefer CSS/Tailwind animations over JavaScript animations
- Use `loading.tsx` and `<Suspense>` for streaming — avoid blocking entire pages on slow data
- Parallel data fetching with `Promise.all` — avoid sequential waterfalls
- Set explicit `cacheLife` profiles on all cached data — don't rely on implicit defaults
- Keep Client Components small and at leaf positions in the tree
- Use route groups to create separate layouts — avoid re-rendering shared UI

## Import Conventions

```tsx
// 1. React/Next.js imports
import { Suspense } from "react";
import Image from "next/image";
import Link from "next/link";
import { redirect } from "next/navigation";

// 2. Third-party imports
import { z } from "zod";

// 3. Internal imports (path aliases)
import { db } from "@/lib/db";
import { auth } from "@/lib/auth";
import { cn } from "@/lib/utils";
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button";

// 4. Relative imports (co-located files)
import { StatsCard } from "./stats-card";

// 5. Types (import type when possible)
import type { Metadata } from "next";
import type { User } from "@/lib/types";
```

### Rules

- Use `@/` path alias for all project imports (configured in tsconfig `paths`)
- Use `import type` for type-only imports — reduces bundle size
- Never use relative paths that go up more than one level (`../../` is a smell — use `@/`)
- Group imports in the order above with blank lines between groups

## Component Structure Convention

```tsx
// 1. Directive (only if needed)
"use client";

// 2. Imports (grouped as above)
import { useState } from "react";
import { cn } from "@/lib/utils";

// 3. Types
interface FeatureCardProps {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  isHighlighted?: boolean;
}

// 4. Component
export function FeatureCard({ title, description, isHighlighted = false }: FeatureCardProps) {
  const [isExpanded, setIsExpanded] = useState(false);

  if (!title) return null;

  return (
    <div className={cn("rounded-lg border p-6", isHighlighted && "border-primary bg-primary/5")}>
      <h3 className="text-lg font-semibold">{title}</h3>
      {isExpanded && <p className="mt-2 text-muted-foreground">{description}</p>}
    </div>
  );
}

// 5. Helper functions (below the component, not above)
```

### Rules

- Named exports for components — default exports only for `page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, `loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`, `not-found.tsx`, `template.tsx`, `global-error.tsx`
- Destructure props in the function signature
- Use early returns for guard clauses
- Keep components under 150 lines — extract sub-components if longer
- Co-locate component-specific types with the component

## Cloudflare Workers Deployment (OpenNext)

### Configuration

```ts
// open-next.config.ts
import { defineCloudflareConfig } from "@opennextjs/cloudflare";
import r2IncrementalCache from "@opennextjs/cloudflare/overrides/incremental-cache/r2-incremental-cache";
import { withRegionalCache } from "@opennextjs/cloudflare/overrides/incremental-cache/regional-cache";

export default defineCloudflareConfig({
  incrementalCache: withRegionalCache(r2IncrementalCache, { mode: "long-lived" }),
});
```

```jsonc
// wrangler.jsonc
{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",
  "name": "cvfairy",
  "compatibility_date": "2024-12-30",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat", "global_fetch_strictly_public"],
  "assets": {
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  },
  "services": [
    { "binding": "WORKER_SELF_REFERENCE", "service": "cvfairy" }
  ],
  "r2_buckets": [
    { "binding": "NEXT_INC_CACHE_R2_BUCKET", "bucket_name": "cvfairy-cache" }
  ],
  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [
      { "name": "NEXT_CACHE_DO_QUEUE", "class_name": "DOQueueHandler" }
    ]
  }
}
```

### Access Cloudflare Bindings

```ts
import { getCloudflareContext } from "@opennextjs/cloudflare";

export async function uploadToR2(file: File) {
  const { env } = await getCloudflareContext();
  await env.UPLOADS.put(file.name, file.stream());
}
```

### Cloudflare-Specific Rules

- Use `@opennextjs/cloudflare` adapter (NOT `@cloudflare/next-on-pages` which is legacy)
- Use R2 for incremental cache with `withRegionalCache` in `long-lived` mode
- DO NOT use KV for incremental cache — it is eventually consistent and causes stale data
- Use Durable Objects for the revalidation queue and tag cache
- `nodejs_compat` flag is required in wrangler.jsonc
- `compatibility_date` must be `2024-09-23` or later
- Worker compressed size limit: 3 MiB (free), 10 MiB (paid)
- Use `getCloudflareContext()` to access bindings (R2, KV, D1, DO) from Server Components and Actions
- Run `npx wrangler types --env-interface CloudflareEnv cloudflare-env.d.ts` to generate binding types
- Use `initOpenNextCloudflareForDev()` in `next.config.ts` for local dev with bindings
- Commands: `npm run preview` (local Workers runtime), `npm run deploy` (deploy to Cloudflare)
- Add `public/_headers` for static asset caching headers
- Add `.open-next` to `.gitignore`

## Metadata and SEO

```tsx
// Static metadata
export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: "Dashboard | CVFairy",
  description: "Manage your CV and job applications",
};

// Dynamic metadata
export async function generateMetadata({ params }: { params: Promise<{ slug: string }> }): Promise<Metadata> {
  const { slug } = await params;
  const post = await getPost(slug);
  return {
    title: post.title,
    description: post.excerpt,
    openGraph: { title: post.title, description: post.excerpt, images: [post.coverImage] },
  };
}
```

### Rules

- Use `metadata` export or `generateMetadata()` in `page.tsx` or `layout.tsx`
- NEVER use `<Head>` from `next/head`
- Always include `title`, `description`, and `openGraph` for public pages
- In Next.js 16, `params` in `generateMetadata` is a `Promise` — must be awaited

## Security Rules

- NEVER hardcode secrets, API keys, or credentials
- NEVER expose server-only env vars to the client (no `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix for secrets)
- NEVER log environment variables or sensitive data
- Always validate and sanitize user input at the boundary (Server Actions, Route Handlers)
- Use parameterized queries — NEVER concatenate user input into SQL
- Authenticate before processing in every Server Action and Route Handler
- Use CSRF protection (built into Server Actions by default)
- Rate-limit public API endpoints
- Set security headers in proxy.ts or wrangler.jsonc

## Do NOT

- Use Pages Router patterns (`getServerSideProps`, `getStaticProps`, `pages/` directory)
- Use `next/router` — always `next/navigation`
- Use `<Head>` from `next/head` — use metadata API
- Use `middleware.ts` — use `proxy.ts` (Next.js 16)
- Use `export const dynamic`, `export const revalidate`, `export const fetchCache` — use Cache Components
- Use `useFormState` from `react-dom` — use `useActionState` from `react`
- Use `forwardRef` — pass refs as regular props (React 19)
- Use `any` type — use `unknown` and narrow
- Use CSS Modules or styled-components — use Tailwind CSS
- Use `useEffect` + `fetch` + `useState` for initial data loading — use Server Components or TanStack Query
- Use `@cloudflare/next-on-pages` — use `@opennextjs/cloudflare`
- Use KV for incremental cache — use R2
- Use single-argument `revalidateTag(tag)` — always pass a cacheLife profile as second arg
